CONTRIBUTING

Contributions are welcome. Thank you for considering one.

To get started:

1. Fork the repository and clone your fork.
2. Set up your environment using the README instructions.

For general advice, see the GitHub docs: https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/exploring-projects-on-github/contributing-to-a-project

You can then start working on your fork.

CONTRIBUTING CODE

First open an issue describing the problem you'd like to address, and get
agreement from the code owners that the feature aligns with the goals and aims of
the project.

I wouldn't want you to spend a lot of effort building a feature that I'm unwilling
to maintain or merge.

Have fun!

MERGE PROTECTION (MAINTAINERS)

Don't merge pull requests into main or release until the `ci` status check passes.
A GitHub ruleset enforces this. The ruleset lives in repository settings rather
than the repo tree, and the intended configuration is checked in at
.github/rulesets/require-ci.json. For how to import and verify it, see
.github/rulesets/README.md.

DEVELOPMENT ENVIRONMENTS

Set up your environment using the README. The docs/ directory documents the
alternatives for end users: Devbox, DDEV, Docker, and local PHP. For a sandboxed,
reproducible environment using Canonical Workshop, see .workshop/README.md.

DEBUGGING WITH XDEBUG

When you debug locally with Xdebug, open the site at http://localhost:8747/ rather
than 127.0.0.1 or another host alias, so the Xdebug client picks up the session
correctly. This applies to all local setups: the built-in PHP server, Devbox, and
DDEV.
